WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.580
Apparently, being locked in a lifelong struggle
between the dark and light sides of the Force

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.200
is really good for your pecs.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.450
There are plenty of shocking moments in Star
Wars: The Last Jedi, but one of the most unexpected

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.871
occurs when Adam Driver, playing erstwhile
villain Kylo Ren, appears shirtless for no

00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:15.871
apparent reason.

00:00:15.871 --> 00:00:20.660
It may not seem like much, but residents of
the Star Wars galaxy rarely show much skin,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.810
except of course of that one time Leia was
forced to wear a metal bikini in Return of

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:25.810
the Jedi.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.190
And we all know how that worked out.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:31.170
So why did writer-director Rian Johnson make
such a bold, beefcakey choice?

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:35.900
In The Last Jedi, Supreme Leader Snoke creates
a Force-driven connection between Kylo Ren

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:36.900
and Rey.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.650
Their minds are combined into the same consciousness
and they're suddenly able to see and hear

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:45.120
each other without actually being in the same
space, an incredible feat matched only by

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.740
the supernatural force known as FaceTime.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.989
It's during one of those mind melds that Rey
catches Kylo Ren shirtless.

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:53.100
"I'd rather not do this right now," she says.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.880
"Yeah, me too," he replies.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.149
If it happened on an episode of The Big Bang
Theory, there would be a laugh track.

00:00:58.149 --> 00:00:59.140
"Howard, listen."

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.140
"No it's your Millenium Falcon.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.020
You and Chewbacca can do whatever you want
to do.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.200
Me and Princess Leia will find some other
way to spend the evening."

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.390
But since nothing in Star Wars happens by
accident - except Boba Fett being eaten by

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.670
the Sarlacc - it turns out Kylo is half-naked
for a reason.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.580
Sound supervisor Ren Klyce told The Huffington
Post that Kylo Ren is shirtless to prove to

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.680
the audience that Rey can actually see him.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.350
"The way in which [director Rian Johnson]
decided to create the Force connection [was]

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:30.270
by just simply doing vertical cuts without
using any CG… it's pure simplicity in terms

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.260
of filmmaking with visual cuts.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.150
We cut to her side; we cut to Kylo Ren; we
cut to her; and back and forth.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.570
[Kylo Ren being shirtless] was important to
establish what she was actually seeing.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.750
Was she hearing his voice or seeing his face
or just his eyes?

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:47.470
And so that [shirtless scene] is to inform
the audience, 'Oh, she can see his body…

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:48.470
it's also good humor."

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.200
Editor Bob Ducsay reiterated that the moment
serves a purpose.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.730
"Over the course of those sequences, you come
to understand all the rules of [the Force

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:56.730
connection].

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.530
Ultimately, it's just good storytelling if
the audience is learning things the same time

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.659
the characters they're following are, instead
of ahead or behind."

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:06.619
So there's a perfectly rational reason for
the wardrobe choice.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:09.810
The question now is, how much does the First
Order charge for gym memberships?

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.160
Thanks for watching!

